{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-44060","model_call":37,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q853"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q853"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_23_332200854#4_753684823","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q853","rank":6,"snippet":"<cover>1984 by George Orwell, and Fidel Castro’s Government in Cuba Figure 1: A Handmaid views the bodies of criminals hanging on the Wall, with their crimes visible for all to see on a plaque on their chests (Wong). Bibliography Cancel reply \" , \"segment\": \"Immediately, the citizens blame Goldstein, the leader of the anti-Big Brother society called the Brotherhood. This renews their mutual hatred of Goldstein, the “primal traitor, [and] the earliest defiler of the party’s purity” (Orwell 12). The Party indoctrinates the citizens to believe that “all subsequent crimes against the Party… sprang directly out of [Goldstein’s] teaching” (Orwell 12), and consequently schedules Two Minute Hates (Orwell 2 Minutes of Hate) each day that feature videos of Goldstein speaking and the mobs of Party members expressing their deep hate of his teachings and their loyalty to the Party. In fact, the Hates work the citizens into such a nationalistic frenzy that “before the hate [s] had proceeded for thirty seconds, uncontrollable exclamations of rage were breaking out from half the people in the room” (Orwell 13). These overt forms of propaganda serve to unite the citizens against a common enemy </cover>and "},"time":15},"model_calls":37,"document_judgments":26,"relevance_3":17,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
