{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-44060","model_call":47,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q855"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q855"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_54_109245095#1_277221339","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q855","rank":11,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_54_109245095#1_277221339\" , \"end_char\": 1377.000000 , \"headings\": \"Principles of Ingsoc Premium Essay Principles of Ingsoc Similar Documents Newspeak Metropolis and 1984 1984 to Today 1984 Orwell Ggfdgfdg The Hell of <cover>1984 \" , \"segment\": \"The main leader of this government was Big Brother. Newspeak was the official language of Oceania. INGSOC is English Socialism according to Newspeak language. There are three principles of INGSOC: War is Peace, Freedom is Slavery and Ignorance is Strength. War is peace is a belief that when a country is at war with some other country, the people and the states of the country get united to fight against their common enemy. </cover>In this novel, government used this principle to maintain peace in their country by being at war with Eurasia. This united the people of Oceania to fight with Eurasia. There are also some other examples of War is peace in this novel; during the two minute hate, when the face of Goldstein appeared on the screen and hate started, there were pictures of some Eurasian ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_54_109245095#1_277221339","rank":11,"relevance":3}]},"time":15},"model_calls":47,"document_judgments":35,"relevance_3":25,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-44060","model_call":47,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q855"},"model_calls":47,"document_judgments":35,"relevance_3":25,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-44060","model_call":47,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q855"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q855"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_23_332200854#3_753681491","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q855","rank":12,"snippet":"<cover>War is Peace.” Essentially, Orwell creates a society in which war and violence serve not only as a way of keeping balance and order, but also to serve as a rallying point for the citizens. For instance, posters that decorate the city center proclaim war with Eurasia, but on a whim, the Party changes the enemy nation to Eastasia and eliminates all traces of the past war with Eurasia. Immediately, the citizens blame Goldstein, the leader of the anti-Big Brother society called the Brotherhood. This renews their mutual hatred of Goldstein, the “primal traitor, [and] the earliest defiler of the party’s purity” (Orwell 12). The Party indoctrinates the citizens to believe that “all subsequent crimes against the Party… sprang directly out of [Goldstein’s] teaching” (Orwell 12), and consequently schedules Two Minute Hates (Orwell 2 Minutes of Hate) each day that feature videos of Goldstein speaking and the mobs of Party members expressing their deep hate of his teachings and their loyalty to the Party. In fact, the Hates work the citizens into such a nationalistic frenzy that “before the hate [s] had proceeded for thirty seconds, uncontrollable exclamations of rage were breaking out from half the people </cover>","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_23_332200854#3_753681491","rank":12,"relevance":3}]},"time":13},"model_calls":47,"document_judgments":35,"relevance_3":25,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
